        <article class="prose max-w-none">
          <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ink mb-4 dark:text-on-primary">Pakistan Sales Tax Guide 2024/25: 18% GST Rates and FBR Filing</h1>
          <p class="text-lg text-body dark:text-muted">Last updated: July 2026 | 9 min read</p>

            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580519542036-c47de6196ba5?w=1200&q=80" alt="Pakistani currency coins and banknotes with tax documents" class="mb-6 w-full rounded-lg object-cover max-h-80 sm:max-h-96" loading="eager" />

            <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-body dark:text-muted">
            <p>Sales tax in Pakistan is a value-added tax (VAT) governed by the Sales Tax Act 1990 and collected by the Federal Board of Revenue (FBR). It is charged at each stage of the supply chain, but registered businesses can recover the tax paid on purchases, so the final burden falls on the consumer. The standard sales tax rate is <strong>18%</strong> on most goods.</p>

            <p>Understanding who must register, how to file monthly returns, and the difference between input tax and output tax is essential for manufacturers, importers, wholesalers, distributors, and retailers. Non-compliance can result in penalties, removal from the Active Taxpayers List, and loss of input tax credits. This guide covers registration thresholds, filing obligations, and how to use a Pakistan sales tax calculator for estimates.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Who Must Register for Sales Tax in Pakistan</h2>
            <p>Registration is mandatory for any person making taxable supplies who falls into specific categories. You must register for sales tax with the FBR and obtain a Sales Tax Registration Number (STRN) if your business meets any of the following criteria:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Manufacturers:</strong> Annual taxable turnover exceeding PKR 10 million</li>
              <li><strong>Importers:</strong> All importers of taxable goods — no turnover threshold</li>
              <li><strong>Wholesalers and distributors:</strong> Dealing in taxable goods</li>
              <li><strong>Tier-1 retailers:</strong> National chains, air-conditioned mall outlets, large shops, or businesses with high electricity bills</li>
              <li><strong>Service providers:</strong> Hotels, clubs, caterers, courier services, and others required under provincial or federal law</li>
              <li><strong>Exporters:</strong> Persons making zero-rated supplies who want to claim refunds</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Registration is done entirely online through the FBR IRIS portal at <a href="https://iris.fbr.gov.pk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">iris.fbr.gov.pk</a>. You need an active National Tax Number (NTN) before applying for an STRN.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Sales Tax Rates in Pakistan</h2>
            <p>The standard sales tax rate is <strong>18%</strong> on most goods. However, certain items attract reduced rates of 10% or 12%, while others are zero-rated or exempt under the Sixth Schedule to the Sales Tax Act 1990. It is important to verify the correct rate for your specific product category, as misapplying rates can trigger audits and penalties.</p>
            <p>Sales tax on services is administered by provincial authorities, not the FBR. Provinces such as Sindh, Punjab, and KPK have their own service tax rates and filing requirements, which are separate from federal sales tax on goods.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Input Tax vs Output Tax</h2>
            <p>The core of Pakistan's sales tax system is the distinction between output tax and input tax. Understanding this mechanism is critical for compliance:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Output tax:</strong> The 18% sales tax you charge and collect from your customers on taxable sales.</li>
              <li><strong>Input tax:</strong> The sales tax you paid on your business purchases and imports, supported by valid tax invoices.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Each month, you deduct admissible input tax from your output tax and pay the FBR the balance. Section 7 of the Sales Tax Act allows this adjustment, while Section 8 lists inputs that are not claimable. Section 8B generally caps input tax claims at <strong>90% of output tax</strong> for the period, with excess carried forward or refunded in eligible cases.</p>
            <p>For example, if your monthly taxable sales total PKR 2,000,000, your output tax is PKR 360,000. If your purchases with input tax total PKR 1,200,000, your input tax is PKR 216,000. Your net sales tax payable to FBR is PKR 144,000.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Monthly Sales Tax Filing Calendar</h2>
            <p>Registered persons must file a sales tax return every month, even if there were no taxable transactions during the period. The standard filing timeline follows a three-date rhythm:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>By the 10th of the following month:</strong> File Annexure C (sales invoices and output tax declaration)</li>
              <li><strong>By the 15th of the following month:</strong> Deposit the payment of tax due</li>
              <li><strong>By the 18th of the following month:</strong> E-file the complete return on the IRIS portal</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Late filing attracts a penalty of PKR 10,000 or 5% of tax payable, whichever is higher. Late payment triggers a default surcharge on the outstanding amount. Beyond rupee penalties, late filing can push you off the Active Taxpayers List, causing your buyers to lose their input tax claim on your invoices.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Exemptions and Zero-Rated Supplies</h2>
            <p>Certain goods and services are exempt from sales tax or charged at a zero rate. Exempt items include computer software, poultry feeds, medicines, and unprocessed agricultural produce as specified in the Sixth Schedule. Zero-rated supplies are taxable at 0%, but the seller can claim input tax credits. Exporters typically deal in zero-rated supplies and must file for refunds when input tax exceeds output tax.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Penalties for Non-Compliance</h2>
            <p>The FBR enforces strict penalties for sales tax defaults. Common violations and their consequences include:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>Failure to register when required: Penalty plus recovery of tax due</li>
              <li>Failure to file monthly returns: PKR 10,000 or 5% of tax payable</li>
              <li>Late payment of tax: Default surcharge on outstanding amounts</li>
              <li>Non-filing or fraudulent returns: Removal from Active Taxpayers List and potential prosecution</li>
            </ul>
            <p>To stay compliant, maintain detailed records of purchases, sales, and tax invoices for at least five years. The FBR may request these during audits or scrutiny proceedings.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Pakistan's sales tax system is designed to ensure transparency at every stage of the supply chain. By registering on time, filing monthly returns by the 18th, and maintaining accurate invoicing records, businesses can claim input tax credits, avoid penalties, and stay on the Active Taxpayers List. Use our <a href="/countries/pakistan/tax/sales-tax-calculator">Pakistan sales tax calculator</a> to estimate your monthly obligations, and for broader tax planning, explore our <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan tax calculators</a>.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the standard sales tax rate in Pakistan?</h3>
            <p>The standard sales tax rate in Pakistan is 18% on most goods under the Sales Tax Act 1990. Reduced rates of 10% and 12% apply to specified items, with zero-rated and exempt categories listed in the schedules.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">When is the monthly sales tax return due?</h3>
            <p>Annexure C must be filed by the 10th, payment made by the 15th, and the complete return e-filed on the <a href="https://iris.fbr.gov.pk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">IRIS portal</a> by the 18th of the following month.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the difference between input tax and output tax?</h3>
            <p>Output tax is the sales tax you charge customers on sales. Input tax is the sales tax you paid on purchases and imports. You pay the FBR the difference between output and input tax each month.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Who must register for sales tax?</h3>
            <p>Manufacturers with turnover above PKR 10 million, all importers, wholesalers, distributors, Tier-1 retailers, service providers, and exporters must register for sales tax with the FBR.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I claim a refund if my input tax exceeds output tax?</h3>
            <p>Yes. If your input tax exceeds output tax due to exports or other zero-rated supplies, you can claim a refund. The FBR must process refunds within 45 days for export-related claims.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What happens if I file my sales tax return late?</h3>
            <p>Late filing attracts a penalty of PKR 10,000 or 5% of tax payable, whichever is higher. You may also be removed from the Active Taxpayers List, which harms your ability to issue tax invoices for input tax credits.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is sales tax on goods the same as sales tax on services?</h3>
            <p>No. Sales tax on goods is federal and administered by the FBR. Sales tax on services is provincial and administered by authorities such as the Sindh Revenue Board (SRB), Punjab Revenue Authority (PRA), or KP Revenue Authority (KPRA). Some businesses need both.</p>
